WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.300
Tell me about the job that you came into when they started the station here.

2
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.480
What was here at the time?

3
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:12.700
Well, I don’t know just exactly what the date was.

4
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:24.840
But when they started radio at LSU, Harley Smith worked with the Speech Department and with the General Extension Division.

5
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:32.920
It’s now Continuing Education, but at that time it was called General Extension under Dr. [Joseph W.] Brouillette.

6
00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:41.220
Harley and Dr. Brouillette had been working in developing this area of radio.

7
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:45.700
One of Harley’s students was Ralph Steetle.

8
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:57.500
So after Harley left, Ralph took over and went full time with the General Extension Division in developing radio.

9
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:05.000
I came here when the thousand-watt FM station was in the planning stage.

10
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.820
I was brought here as Program Director of that FM station.

11
00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:12.800
I wore two hats.

12
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:19.640
I taught half-time in the Speech Department . . . broadcasting courses in the Speech Department.

13
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:29.320
Then I was half-time with General Extension in production as Program Director of the radio station.

14
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.040
So that’s how I came to Louisiana.

15
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:39.540
Well, you were here a while working under Steetle at the radio station.

16
00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:39.740
Yes.

17
00:01:39.607 --> 00:01:39.807
Yes.

18
00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:42.420
I was around at that time then, too.

19
00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.460
I remember Steetle very well.

20
00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:48.100
I think he was a Midwesterner.

21
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:49.700
He was from Ohio, or something.

22
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.100
He was from Ohio.

23
00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:55.880
And later became head of educational television for the state of Oregon.

24
00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:56.530
That’s right.

25
00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:00.840
He’s now retired from there.

26
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:08.740
Tell me some of the problems that you faced in those days . . . Very little help, I’m sure.

27
00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:19.240
Well, of course, there were a number of universities who had radio stations at that time.

28
00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.780
They were AM stations.

29
00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:27.020
I worked with the one at the University of Iowa when I was there.

30
00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:33.120
Wisconsin, Iowa, Iowa State College, now Iowa State University,

31
00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:40.680
Illinois . . . were some that just come to my mind that had operating AM stations at that time.

32
00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:54.780
But LSU . . . Baton Rouge did not have an AM frequency available at that time for an AM station for education.

33
00:02:54.780 --> 00:03:10.360
So when FM frequencies were set aside for the universities and other educational institutions, they decided that LSU should have such a station.

34
00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.420
That was when Ralph and Dr. Brouillette started building one.
